{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794552659,"nonce":"zMVRw7q9Eszrl93U91VysgYk9kw=","message":"Landed locally in 241a0233736a193aa6084a044a2a1ba6a8b97bcc (not pushed). A FLIP slide is the translate of a motion binding the animation makes for the node (AnimatedValue::drive, no spring, target 0), refreshed each frame from tick_flip_animations; the node is painted where layout put it and the existing rows path moves it. Used when the renderer moves bound nodes by rows (not baked) and the node has no binding of the application's; otherwise the slide stays in the props with a full paint, as before. The binding is released when the slide ends or the node is replaced, and the frame after is a full paint (rows decline). The window, the compositor and the CSS composite check ask has_flip_animations_off_rows, so the fast path stays open. Harness: animation_paths scenes flip (text and none) and a 30-tile grid, each tile its own binding: 17 of 22 to 24 frames by rows (one full paint when layout changes, one when the binding ends), 0 pixels off the full paint on CPU rows and the table, and a test that the cards move and rows carry them. Frame cost in the harness (dev, includes the GPU readback floor): 3.3 ms painted in full against 1.9 ms by rows, flat from 20 to 90 tiles. The harness now paints in full after a layout, as a window does, and tests read a slide's drawn place through RenderTree::flip_offset. Not done: the iOS and Android runners still paint in full for any FLIP; layout and visual animations are not folded in (they change layout each frame); the slide is evaluated on the CPU each frame (P2b would evaluate it on the GPU); not looked at in a real window.","files":null}]}